{
  "data": {
    "title": "Sleep/Wake Regularity Associated with Default Mode Network Structure among Healthy Adolescents and Young Adults",
    "text": "Title: Sleep/Wake Regularity Associated with Default Mode Network Structure among Healthy Adolescents and Young Adults\n\nConnectivity matrices\nCONN toolbox ROI-to-ROI analyses (v18.b) in conjunction with SPM12 were used to generate connectivity matrices. The regions of interest used a human connectome project template atlas of intrinsic resting-state networks, including 32 regions over 6 networks. ROI-level time courses were created by averaging voxels within each ROI, and connectivity was defined by Fisher’s transformed bivariate correlation coefficients.\n\nGraph theory analyses\nROI-to-ROI graph metrics including path length and clustering coefficient were calculated using graph theory analyses. Adjacency matrix edges were weighted by cost values over 0.15 with a two-sided FDR-correction threshold of p < 0.05. Path length reflects the average minimum path distance between nodes, while clustering coefficient reflects the degree of interconnectedness within the network."
  },
